Description: Check the maximum inscribed circle without fixing a non-unique center
 A 10 by 20 rectangle has multiple radius-five maximum circles. New GEOS
 versions choose a different center. Assert the radius, that its disk is
 covered by the polygon, and that the segment ends on the boundary.
 Keep both integer and floating tolerance inputs.
Author: Ruohang Feng <rh@vonng.com>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

--- a/test/sql_stmt_geos3100_tests/geosmaxcircle6.testcase
+++ b/test/sql_stmt_geos3100_tests/geosmaxcircle6.testcase
@@ -1,7 +1,11 @@
 GEOSMaximumInscribedCircle - VALID BLOB  Geometry
 :memory: #use in-memory database
-SELECT AsText(GEOSMaximumInscribedCircle(GeomFromText('POLYGON ((0 0, 10 0, 10 20, 0 20, 0 0))'), 0.5))
+WITH input(p) AS (SELECT GeomFromText('POLYGON ((0 0, 10 0, 10 20, 0 20, 0 0))')), result(p, g) AS (SELECT p, GEOSMaximumInscribedCircle(p, 0.5) FROM input) SELECT ST_Length(g) AS radius, ST_Covers(p, ST_Buffer(ST_StartPoint(g), ST_Length(g))) AS circle_is_covered, ST_Intersects(ST_Boundary(p), ST_EndPoint(g)) AS end_is_on_boundary FROM result
 1 # rows (not including the header row)
-1 # columns
-AsText(GEOSMaximumInscribedCircle(GeomFromText('POLYGON ((0 0, 10 0, 10 20, 0 20, 0 0))'), 0.5))
-LINESTRING(5 10, 10 10)
+3 # columns
+radius
+circle_is_covered
+end_is_on_boundary
+5.0
+1
+1
